                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_process:       `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> The threat is transactional friction, not the product or the price — buyers want to order, see stock and get pricing digitally, and your specification expertise still commands a premium. Digitizing the process answers the mandate while freeing that expertise to scale.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Careful — this is not a price problem.</strong> Match the pure-plays and every account expects the same: hand back 8% on €184M and that's €14M, more than twice your whole budget. What were customers actually paying the premium <em>for</em>? Protect the value of expertise, don't surrender it.`,
                    coach_r1_product:       `<strong>Your components aren't the moat — your people are.</strong> Treating this as a commoditized-product problem points you at the slow, expensive product layer and misses the immediate mandate. The 38-strong technical team is what rivals can't copy.`,
                    coach_r1_nothing:       `<strong>Hanssen is not bluffing.</strong> The portal goes live in Q3 and eleven more accounts have flagged the same demand. 'Ride it out' loses €10.4M from one account alone. There absolutely is a structural threat — name it.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the threat as transactional friction AND hypothesized the process layer first — the one layer that meets the 1 September deadline and amplifies, rather than commoditizes, the technical-applications moat. That is exactly the lens this case rewards.`,
                    coach_r1_spread:        `<strong>You can't digitize all three at depth.</strong> €6.0M split three ways funds none properly — the portal ships late and misses the cutoff, the IoT product launches under-instrumented. Digital innovation is a layered choice, not a spreading of bets.`,
                    coach_r1_lowmoat:       `<strong>You haven't named what you're defending.</strong> If the brief doesn't centre the technical-applications expertise, you'll digitize toward the commodity middle where the pure-plays already win. Name the moat before you spend a euro.`,
                    coach_r2_process:       `<strong>The process layer answers the ultimatum on the table.</strong> At €2.6M and six months it ships before 1 September, satisfies Hanssen and the eleven contagion accounts, and is scalable to all 3,200 accounts. It also frees reps from order-taking to sell the expertise.`,
                    coach_r2_business:      `<strong>Highest margin, wrong sequence.</strong> Managed-inventory subscription takes 18 months and €4.4M. Hanssen deprioritizes you in six — you'd lose ~€10M before the new model earns its first euro. What protects the cash this year so you can afford the model next year?`,
                    coach_r2_product:       `<strong>The product layer is slow.</strong> Fourteen months and €3.8M defends premium on ~20% of SKUs, but it does not answer Hanssen's API mandate due 1 September. You've protected a slice and lost the account that triggered all this.`,
                    coach_r2_underfund:     `<strong>You picked process but starved it.</strong> Below €2.6M the portal and pricing engine ship late and miss the 1 September cutoff anyway — you've spent budget and still failed the mandate. Fund the layer that beats the deadline to depth.`,
                    coach_r2_overcap:       `<strong>That breaks the €6.0M ceiling.</strong> The board fixed one budget and there is no top-up. A lead plus a partial second bet must fit inside €6.0M — over-reach now means you can't fund the sequenced pivot later.`,
                    coach_r2_disciplined:   `<strong>Disciplined portfolio call.</strong> You funded the process layer to depth to beat the deadline, sequenced a partial second bet, and named the trade-off out loud — you are deliberately not funding the subscription at depth this year. That is what 'one shot' demands.`,
                    coach_r2_deny:          `<strong>You can't do it all for €6.0M.</strong> Promising the board you'll fund every layer at depth is exactly Common Error 1 — you'll under-resource the one thing that beats the deadline. Name the trade-off; a real strategy says what you are <em>not</em> doing.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced self-service target.</strong> Routing the routine half of orders to digital frees rep capacity without stranding the technical team — that's the migration that adds revenue rather than just relocating it.`,
                    coach_r3_toofast:       `<strong>Too far, too fast.</strong> Pushing most orders to self-service strands the 38-person technical team and trains customers to see you as a commodity catalogue. The moat erodes precisely where you needed it to amplify.`,
                    coach_r3_tooslow:       `<strong>That barely moves the channel.</strong> Leaving almost everything in assisted channels means the digital share never reaches the benchmark and Hanssen still can't self-serve. Free some routine volume so reps can sell the expertise.`,
                    coach_r3_reallocate:    `<strong>This is the reallocation done right.</strong> A balanced self-service share plus a real block of reps moved from order-taking to technical solution selling is what turns freed capacity into defended differentiation — not just lower cost.`,
                    coach_r3_toomany:       `<strong>You moved too many heads too soon.</strong> Stripping order-taking before self-service can absorb it leaves accounts unserved and margin exposed. Sequence the headcount shift to the adoption curve.`,
                    coach_r3_noreps:        `<strong>You digitized the process but left the sales force untouched.</strong> If no reps move toward solution selling, the freed capacity is wasted and the expertise advantage never scales. Reallocation is the point, not just the portal.`,
                    coach_r3_undercut:      `<strong>You started a price war you can't win.</strong> Undercutting the pure-plays by 8% surrenders the margin that funds your technical team — the very thing that differentiates you. The pure-plays have a lower cost base; on price, they win.`,
                    coach_r3_mandate:       `<strong>Mandate-and-police is not buy-in.</strong> Order reps to push digital while their commission is tied to order volume and they'll quietly steer customers back to phone. Adoption stalls. Change how they're paid and measured, don't just police them.`,
                    coach_r3_noconflict:    `<strong>'They'll adapt' is how adoption stalls at 11%.</strong> Reps whose pay depends on order volume have every reason to undermine the portal. Without a mechanism — credit and commission redesign — digital just relocates revenue and inflames conflict.`,
                    coach_r4_adoption:      `<strong>That's the right governance spine.</strong> Digital share, customer adoption and margin impact at 6/12/18 months give you leading and lagging signals — you'll see the change slipping before the revenue confirms it, and you can prove it's real.`,
                    coach_r4_revenue:       `<strong>Revenue alone is too slow and too thin.</strong> A quarterly revenue number won't warn you when adoption decays, and it says nothing about whether you protected the moat or the margin. Measure adoption and margin, not just the top line.`,
                    coach_r4_launch:        `<strong>A launch dashboard then moving on is the classic decay pattern.</strong> 'We built a portal' is an output, not an outcome. Eighteen months from now, how does the board know it worked? Governance has to outlive the go-live.`,
                    coach_r4_value:         `<strong>That's how you defend the edge.</strong> You held price and showed Hanssen the integrated availability and specification value the pure-play can't deliver — digitization amplifying the moat, not a race to the bottom. The 8% gap is the pure-play's problem, not yours.`,
                    coach_r4_partial:       `<strong>Conceding 4% sets the anchor.</strong> Split the difference once and every account expects it next — you've started eroding the premium that funds the technical team. Defend the value, don't negotiate against your own moat.`,
                    coach_r4_match:         `<strong>Matching the 8% cut surrenders the war.</strong> Hand back 8% on €184M and that's €14M — more than twice your transformation budget — and your technical team becomes a cost you can't fund. You kept the account and lost the business model.`,
                    coach_r4_nopivot:       `<strong>No pivot trigger means no governance.</strong> Committing fully with no defined number to redirect the reserve is a bet, not a portfolio. Name the metric and the month that tells you to fund the next layer.`,

